1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,050
The internet ran out of addresses.

2
00:00:02,050 --> 00:00:03,223
Not in theory.

3
00:00:03,223 --> 00:00:09,023
On the third of February 2011, the last free blocks were handed out, at a ceremony in Miami.

4
00:00:09,023 --> 00:00:10,507
And yet you're watching this.

5
00:00:10,507 --> 00:00:14,918
Your phone is online. So is your TV, your laptop, maybe even your fridge.

6
00:00:14,918 --> 00:00:18,222
So how does an internet with no addresses left keep growing?

7
00:00:18,222 --> 00:00:23,335
The answer involves a guess from 1977, a trick hiding in your router,

8
00:00:23,335 --> 00:00:28,330
a market where addresses sell for twenty dollars each, and a fix that took thirty years.

9
00:00:28,330 --> 00:00:33,724
Every device on the internet needs an address, like a phone number, so data knows where to go.

10
00:00:33,724 --> 00:00:39,976
In 1977, Vint Cerf, one of the engineers who designed the internet's core protocols,

11
00:00:39,976 --> 00:00:42,523
had to decide how long those addresses would be.

12
00:00:42,523 --> 00:00:48,582
Some colleagues wanted 128 bits. Others called that crazy. Cerf picked 32.

13
00:00:48,582 --> 00:00:52,386
Here's the reasoning. They needed a few bits to say which network you're on.

14
00:00:52,386 --> 00:00:56,422
How many countries were there? Nobody knew, and there was no Google to check.

15
00:00:56,422 --> 00:01:03,489
So they guessed 128, doubled it for room, and got 256 networks. That's eight bits.

16
00:01:03,489 --> 00:01:07,283
The other twenty-four bits named the computer inside each network.

17
00:01:07,283 --> 00:01:13,056
Thirty-two bits gives you two to the thirty-second power: about 4.3 billion addresses,

18
00:01:13,056 --> 00:01:18,990
roughly one for every person on Earth at the time. For a research experiment, that was enormous.

19
00:01:18,990 --> 00:01:23,714
Cerf has joked about it ever since. His summary: it was enough for an experiment.

20
00:01:23,714 --> 00:01:26,263
The problem is, the experiment never ended.

21
00:01:26,263 --> 00:01:29,005
For years, addresses were handed out generously.

22
00:01:29,005 --> 00:01:34,161
Early companies and universities got blocks of nearly seventeen million addresses each.

23
00:01:34,161 --> 00:01:36,968
Then came the web, then broadband, then smartphones.

24
00:01:36,968 --> 00:01:41,210
The world's address pool is managed by an organisation called IANA,

25
00:01:41,210 --> 00:01:44,314
which hands big blocks to five regional registries:

26
00:01:44,314 --> 00:01:48,313
North America, Europe, Asia-Pacific, Latin America and Africa.

27
00:01:48,313 --> 00:01:53,563
Each block is one two-hundred-and-fifty-sixth of all IPv4 addresses.

28
00:01:53,563 --> 00:02:00,327
At the end of January 2011, IANA gave two of its last blocks to Asia-Pacific. That left five.

29
00:02:00,327 --> 00:02:05,020
A policy agreed years earlier kicked in: one final block for each region.

30
00:02:05,020 --> 00:02:09,140
On the third of February, they were handed out. The central pool was empty.

31
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:13,556
Then the regions ran dry, one by one. Asia-Pacific within ten weeks.

32
00:02:13,556 --> 00:02:17,659
Europe rationed its last block for years, handing out tiny slices,

33
00:02:17,659 --> 00:02:24,679
until the twenty-fifth of November 2019, when it made its final allocation and announced it had completely run out.

34
00:02:24,679 --> 00:02:29,087
So how are billions of new devices still getting online? Look at your router.

35
00:02:29,087 --> 00:02:33,709
Your home probably has one public address, the one the rest of the internet sees.

36
00:02:33,709 --> 00:02:40,630
Every device inside gets a private address instead, from ranges that are reused in millions of homes at once.

37
00:02:40,630 --> 00:02:46,417
That's why so many home networks use addresses starting with 192.168.

38
00:02:46,417 --> 00:02:52,767
When your phone asks a website for something, the router swaps your phone's private address for the home's public one,

39
00:02:52,767 --> 00:02:55,527
and writes down a port number to remember who asked.

40
00:02:55,527 --> 00:03:00,111
When the answer comes back, it checks its table and passes the answer to your phone.

41
00:03:00,111 --> 00:03:05,584
This is network address translation, or NAT. One public address, dozens of devices.

42
00:03:05,584 --> 00:03:12,950
It works so well that many internet providers now do it a second time, sharing one public address across many customers.

43
00:03:12,950 --> 00:03:14,692
That's called carrier-grade NAT.

44
00:03:14,692 --> 00:03:19,547
But it has a cost. Devices behind NAT can't easily be reached from the outside.

45
00:03:19,547 --> 00:03:28,727
It's why some online games complain about a strict NAT type, why hosting a server at home can be a headache, and why peer-to-peer apps need clever workarounds.

46
00:03:28,727 --> 00:03:31,893
Scarcity did something else: it gave addresses a price.

47
00:03:31,893 --> 00:03:38,473
Companies with spare blocks sell them to companies that need them, through the registries' official transfer process.

48
00:03:38,473 --> 00:03:43,099
In 2026, the average price has been around twenty dollars per address.

49
00:03:43,099 --> 00:03:48,241
A small block of 256 addresses costs several thousand dollars.

50
00:03:48,241 --> 00:03:54,304
Prices have actually fallen over the past year. But the market exists because the old system is full.

51
00:03:54,304 --> 00:04:02,482
The real fix was designed in the 1990s: IPv6. Instead of 32 bits, it uses 128.

52
00:04:02,482 --> 00:04:06,955
That isn't four times as many addresses. Each extra bit doubles the count,

53
00:04:06,955 --> 00:04:11,834
so it's two to the hundred-and-twenty-eighth: about 340 undecillion.

54
00:04:11,834 --> 00:04:20,148
That's 34 followed by 37 zeros. Enough to give every grain of sand on Earth billions of billions of addresses each.

55
00:04:20,148 --> 00:04:26,670
An IPv6 address looks different too: groups of hexadecimal digits separated by colons.

56
00:04:26,670 --> 00:04:33,984
So why didn't everyone just switch? Because IPv6 can't talk directly to IPv4. They're separate languages.

57
00:04:33,984 --> 00:04:39,151
Every website, router, phone and app had to learn the new one while keeping the old one running.

58
00:04:39,151 --> 00:04:41,438
For years, nobody wanted to go first.

59
00:04:41,438 --> 00:04:48,365
Websites didn't bother because users weren't on IPv6, and providers didn't bother because websites weren't.

60
00:04:48,365 --> 00:04:51,544
And NAT made the pain bearable, so the deadline kept slipping.

61
00:04:51,544 --> 00:04:58,636
But it kept moving. Mobile networks adopted it early, because they had millions of new phones and no addresses to give them.

62
00:04:58,636 --> 00:05:03,310
In several countries, most users already connect over IPv6.

63
00:05:03,310 --> 00:05:06,678
And in March 2026, something symbolic happened:

64
00:05:06,678 --> 00:05:12,271
for the first time, Google measured more than half of its users reaching it over IPv6.

65
00:05:12,271 --> 00:05:15,218
Fifty point one percent, on the twenty-eighth of March.

66
00:05:15,218 --> 00:05:21,217
So, did the internet run out of addresses? Yes. In 2011, and then in region after region.

67
00:05:21,217 --> 00:05:22,822
But it didn't stop. It bent.

68
00:05:22,822 --> 00:05:30,306
One address shared by a whole home, then by a whole neighbourhood, while a far bigger address system was slowly built alongside.

69
00:05:30,306 --> 00:05:32,729
Your phone may well be using both right now.

70
00:05:32,729 --> 00:05:38,085
One decision from 1977 still shapes how every device you own connects.

71
00:05:38,085 --> 00:05:44,356
Vint Cerf's own summary is still the best one: it was enough for an experiment. The experiment just never ended.
